## Git Version
Syntax:
git --version

Example:
git version 2.55.0.windows.5

## Git Clone
Purpose: Clone a remote repository to the local machine.

Steps
Open the repository on GitHub.
Click Code.
Select HTTPS.
Copy the repository URL.
Run the git clone command in Git Bash.

Syntax:
```
git clone <repository-URL>
```
Example:
git clone https://github.com/gopikadeviga/git-journey.git

This creates a local copy of the git-journey repository on your computer.

## Git status
Purpose:Check the current status of the working directory and see what changes have been made.

Syntax:
```
git status
```
## Git add
Purpose: Add changes from the working directory to the staging area so they can be included in the next commit.

Syntax:
```
git add --all 
```
(or)
```
git add -A
```
Adds all changes in the repository to the staging area, including:
New files,
Modified files,
Deleted files.

Syntax:
```
git add . 
```
Stages changes from the current directory and its subdirectories.
It is commonly used to stage all changes within the current project directory.

Note: git add . and git add -A are not always exactly the same, especially when dealing with deletions and the location from which the command is run.

Syntax:
```
git add * 
```
Stages new or modified files, not the deleted ones.

Syntax:
```
git add <File> 
```
Stages the specific file.

Example:
git add one.txt
(or)
git add myFolder/two.txt

To stage all the '.txt' files
git add *.txt

## Git remove
Syntax:
1.git rm <filename>

Example: git rm one.txt
Deletes one.txt file and also stages it at the same time.

2.git rm -f <filename>
Forcefull deletion even if any uncommited changes are made.

3.git rm --cached <filename>
Removes files from the staging area, but will not be deleted. Still will be present in the desktop but untracked.

4.git rm -r <folder>
Recursive removal - Removes all the subfolders or files in it recursively

## Git reset
Syntax:
1.git reset
Purpose: Unstage changes that have been added to the staging area.
This moves the changes from the staging area back to the working directory without deleting the changes.

2.git reset HEAD~
Purpose: Undo the last commit while keeping the changes in the working directory.
The last commit is removed from the current branch, but the changes made in that commit are kept so they can be modified or committed again.

3.git reset --hard
Purpose: Remove staged and unstaged changes and make the working directory match the current commit.

### Git config
Purpose: Configure your Git username and email address. Git uses this information to identify the author of commits.

## Global Configuration
Global configuration applies to all repositories for the current user on the computer.

Syntax:
```
git config --global user.email "<email id>"
git config --global user.name "<name>"
```
## Local Configuration
Local configuration applies only to the current repository.

Syntax:
```
git config --local user.email "<email>"
```
### Git commit
Purpose: Save the staged changes as a commit in the local Git repository.

Syntax:
```
git commit -m "<commit message>"
```

Example:
git commit -m "I have made some changes to files"

Note: Before committing, changes need to be added to the staging area using "git add".

## Git log
Syntax
git log
Gives the full commit history with details

git log --oneline
Gives only essential online info..
